          "passage": "In her 2021 book All That She Carried: The Journey of Ashley's Sack, a Black Family Keepsake, historian Tiya Miles, who teaches U.S. history at Harvard, reconstructs the story of a single embroidered cloth sack that passed through three generations of an enslaved Black family in the American South. Working from the brief lines of text stitched onto the sack and from scattered archival fragments, Miles traces how an enslaved mother filled it with a few belongings for her young daughter on the eve of their forced separation at a slave sale. By following the sack across decades, Miles argues that humble objects of this kind functioned as deliberate vehicles of remembrance, allowing Black women under slavery to preserve memory and family ties even as auctions and removals scattered them, and she positions the sack at the center of the material culture of slavery.",
