          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "In her 2016 book From the War on Poverty to the War on Crime, the Yale historian Elizabeth Hinton challenges a familiar account of how the United States arrived at mass incarceration. On the standard telling, federal anti-poverty programs launched under President Lyndon B. Johnson's Great Society in the mid-1960s were later derailed by a separate, conservative War on Crime that vastly expanded federal investment in policing and prisons. Drawing on previously underused records from federal crime, urban, and welfare agencies, Hinton instead traces a continuous policy line from the earliest Great Society legislation through the carceral expansions of the next two decades. The same legislation that funded job training and community programs in low-income, predominantly Black urban neighborhoods, she shows, also routed federal money and personnel into local police departments and reframed urban poverty as a problem of crime control. Mass incarceration, on her account, was not a reversal of the Great Society but its continuation by other means.",
