          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) hooks observed that the mainstream mid-twentieth-century U.S. feminist movement consistently took middle-class white women's experiences as its central frame of reference when defining what counted as a feminist concern, and (2) hooks also stressed that the historical situation of Black women in the United States — shaped by the joint legacies of slavery, segregation, and gendered labor — differed in kind from that frame of reference. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that a political agenda built on the white-middle-class frame would tend to leave the issues specific to Black women's distinct historical situation outside the scope of what it set out to address. Choice B states exactly that hedged claim, using \"tend to\" and \"outside the scope of issues it set out to address\" to capture the structural omission without overreaching.\n\nThe inference combines both premises. Premise (1) alone, about the agenda's reference point, does not by itself say what is left out. Premise (2) alone, about the distinctness of Black women's historical situation, does not by itself describe how the agenda would behave. Only the joint claim — that an agenda derived from one frame of reference would systematically fail to take up concerns rooted in the other situation — is licensed.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (Scope shift): Pushes the modest claim that an agenda would not address Black women's distinct concerns into the absolute claim that it would be \"incapable of producing reforms that benefited Black women in any area of public life.\" The premises license an inference about what the agenda's defined scope would omit, not a sweeping claim that no reform produced under it could ever benefit Black women in any domain. \"Any area\" overreaches the evidence.\n- Choice C (Surface match): Sounds consistent with the passage because the word \"middle-class\" appears in premise (1), so a reader may assume that class is being foregrounded over race. But the premises only identify whose experience served as the frame of reference; they do not establish a comparison between how much attention the agenda paid to class inequality versus race inequality. A comparative ranking is not licensed by the premises, even though it is consistent with one phrase in them.\n- Choice D (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (1). The passage states that the mainstream feminist movement took middle-class white women's experience as its central reference point — so an agenda derived from that frame would not, by its own structure, treat Black women's historical situation as its most central problem. Choice D inverts the direction of the inference."
