          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In her 2012 essay \"Decolonization Is Not a Metaphor,\" the education scholar Eve Tuck, who teaches Indigenous studies at the University of Toronto, has argued that the term decolonization carries a specific obligation that other reform vocabularies do not. On her account, what distinguishes decolonization from related projects of diversification or institutional reform is that it names a material claim: the return of stolen Indigenous land to Indigenous peoples. When educators and theorists nevertheless deploy decolonize to describe efforts that broaden a curriculum or revise an institution's policies, those efforts may be valuable on their own terms, but they leave the land question entirely unaddressed. Tuck therefore concludes that, whatever else such reform projects accomplish, by adopting decolonize as a stand-in for diversify they ______",
