          "passage": "Literary theorist Linda Hutcheon, writing on what she calls historiographic metafiction, argues that postmodern novels of the late twentieth century proceed by a paradoxical double gesture. On one hand, such works carefully reproduce the apparatus of traditional historical realism: dated chapter headings, period-correct detail, and explicit citation of archival documents. On the other hand, the same works deliberately disrupt that apparatus from within — narrators confess to inventing their sources, footnotes contradict the body of the text, and chronology fractures along self-conscious lines. Because neither half of this strategy is incidental to the novels' design, Hutcheon's account implies that any critical reading that ______",
