          "explanation": "The first sentence reports what mid-twentieth-century Western developmental psychology predicted: that mothers facing extremely high infant mortality would respond with an intense, universal grief reaction whenever a child died. The second sentence reports what Scheper-Hughes actually documented in Death Without Weeping (1992): women in a Northeast Brazilian shantytown developed a culturally elaborated emotional restraint, withholding deep attachment from frail newborns so that maternal care could be directed toward infants more likely to survive. The relation between the two sentences is replacement — the predicted response did not occur, and a different response occurred in its place. 'Instead,' is the CB-lexicon transition that marks 'X was expected; Y happened in its place,' and is correct (C).\n\nThe cue that selects 'Instead,' is the explicit substitution in sentence 2: emotional restraint is reported as what the women developed in place of the predicted intense grief, not as an additional finding alongside it.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (A) 'Therefore,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) signals a causal consequence drawn from sentence 1. A reader might loosely hear 'because Western researchers expected universal grief, Scheper-Hughes had to study a Brazilian shantytown,' which gives 'Therefore,' a faint plausibility. But sentence 2 is not presented as a conclusion that follows from the prediction; it reports a finding that overturns it. The relation is replacement, not causal.\n- (B) 'Similarly,' (inverse — opposite logical move) signals that sentence 2 reaches the same kind of conclusion as sentence 1. That is exactly wrong here: Scheper-Hughes is not echoing the universal-grief prediction; her shantytown evidence contradicts it by showing culturally elaborated restraint. 'Similarly,' would point in the wrong direction.\n- (D) 'Moreover,' (scope shift — wrong relation) would frame Scheper-Hughes's finding as an additional point reinforcing the Western prediction. But emotional restraint directed toward selective care is not an additional confirmation of universal intense grief; it is a substitute response that displaces the predicted one. An addition transition cannot mark a sentence whose content replaces, rather than extends, the preceding claim."
