          "domain": "craft-and-structure",
          "skill": "words-in-context",
          "passage": "Cultural theorist Stuart Hall, who directed the Centre for Contemporary Cultural Studies at the University of Birmingham, argued in his 1973 essay \"Encoding/Decoding\" that the apparent transparency of a television message — the sense that its meaning is simply self-evident on the screen — is in fact ______ a body of cultural codes that producers and audiences happen to share. Where those codes diverge, Hall observed, the message that decoders construct departs noticeably from the reading its producers had intended.",
          "question": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "id": "A",
              "text": "consonant with"
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "text": "predicated on"
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "text": "intelligible to"
            },
            {
              "id": "D",
              "text": "incidental to"
            }
          ],
          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"predicated on.\" The sentence containing the blank sets up a tight argument: what looks like the self-evident, transparent meaning of a television message is, on Hall's account, only made possible because producers and audiences share a body of cultural codes. The follow-up sentence confirms the relation by describing what happens when those codes diverge — decoders construct a message that departs from the producers' intended reading, which means the apparent transparency was resting on the shared codes all along. The blank therefore needs a phrase that names a relation of foundational dependence: the felt transparency is built on, and exists only because of, the shared codes. \"Predicated on\" carries exactly that academic-register meaning — to say one thing is predicated on another is to say the first rests on the second as its logical and constitutive premise. Hall's claim in \"Encoding/Decoding\" is precisely that television's apparent self-evidence is the surface effect of a deeper agreement in cultural codes, which is the relation \"predicated on\" names.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: scope shift — \"consonant with\" lives in the same scholarly register as the correct answer and is reached for in academic prose to describe a fit between elements of an account, so it feels at home in a sentence about cultural codes and televisual meaning. Its scope, however, is parallel rather than foundational. To be consonant with something is to be in harmony or agreement with it, two terms that align without one resting on the other. The passage is not claiming that the apparent transparency of a television message merely matches a body of cultural codes; it is claiming that the transparency is brought into being by the shared codes and disappears when they diverge. The phrase has the right register without the right logical relation.\n- Choice C: surface match — \"intelligible to\" picks up the cognitive vocabulary already running through the sentence (\"transparency,\" \"meaning,\" \"self-evident\"), so the phrase feels primed by the surrounding language. But \"intelligible to\" names a relation between a message and a comprehender — something is intelligible to a reader or viewer who can understand it. The blank does not connect the apparent transparency to a comprehender; it connects the transparency to \"a body of cultural codes,\" which is not the kind of thing a message is intelligible to. The phrase collocates with the surface vocabulary while putting the wrong kind of object on the other side of the relation.\n- Choice D: inverse — \"incidental to\" reverses the relation Hall is describing. To say the apparent transparency is incidental to the shared codes would be to say that the codes are peripheral or non-essential to the felt self-evidence of the message — that television looks transparent regardless of whether producers and audiences share codes. The follow-up sentence rules that reading out: when the codes diverge, the apparent transparency breaks down and the decoded message departs from the encoded one. The codes are not incidental to the effect; they are what makes it possible. The phrase points the dependency in exactly the wrong direction."
