          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The passage moves in two clear beats. First, the narrator records a sequence of unsettling impressions from his evening walk: there is no moon, the Zhao family's dog stands watching with eyes that are \"not the eyes of a dog,\" and the villagers turn their heads one after another, as if a single instruction were making its way down the lane behind him. Second, after he has come home, he takes down an old history and reads by the lamp; between the familiar lines about benevolence and right conduct, he has begun to make out two other words, faintly written, that no one before him has thought to look for. The closing sentence — \"I know now what the village has been eating\" — shows that the rereading at the lamp has hardened the walk's impressions into a definite conviction. The structure of the text is therefore the movement from outdoor observations gathered uneasily to a private rereading that turns that unease into certainty.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: surface match — the dog's gaze and the villagers' turned heads are real features of the walk, so a hurried reader can mistake those details for the structure of the whole. But the walk's observations are only the first half of the passage; the structural work of the second half is done by the rereading of the old history, which B does not name. The passage is built around two movements, and B describes only one of them while pretending to describe both.\n- Choice C: inverse — this reverses the order. The narrator's conclusion about the village (\"I know now what the village has been eating\") is the closing sentence; it is reached, not pre-stated, and the walk's observations come before it, not after. C inverts the direction of the movement, treating the result of the passage as its premise.\n- Choice D: scope shift — the rereading of the old history is one movement of the text, but the passage opens and is largely set outdoors, on a walk past the Zhao gate and through the lane. Treating the passage as confined to a single rereading at the lamp narrows its structure to its second half and leaves the walk — which is what the rereading is hardening into conviction — entirely outside the description."
