          "domain": "craft-and-structure",
          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Joseph Conrad's 1899 novel \"Heart of Darkness.\" An unnamed narrator and four companions, among them a seaman named Marlow, are aboard the cruising yawl Nellie, anchored on the Thames at dusk and waiting for the tide to turn.\n\nThe Nellie swung at her anchor without the flutter of a sail, and rested. The tide had made, the wind was nearly calm, and being bound down the river, the only thing for it was to come to and wait for the turn. The sea-reach of the Thames stretched before us like the beginning of an interminable waterway, and in the offing the sea and the sky were welded together without a joint. Marlow sat cross-legged on the deck, leaning against the mizzen-mast, and he had the pose of an idol; for a long while he said nothing at all. Then, in the slow voice of a man not much given to speaking, and with his eyes still fixed upon the brown current that ran past us toward the sea, he observed that this also had been one of the dark places of the earth.",
