          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The text falls into two clearly marked halves. In the first, the narrator describes the still scene around the anchored yawl: the Nellie swings at her anchor without the flutter of a sail, the tide has made, the wind is nearly calm, the sea-reach of the Thames stretches before them like the beginning of an interminable waterway, and the sea and the sky are welded together without a joint. In the second, after a long silence, Marlow — sitting cross-legged on the deck against the mizzen-mast in the pose of an idol — offers his first reflective remark about the river: in the slow voice of a man not much given to speaking, and with his eyes fixed on the brown current that runs past them toward the sea, he observes that this also had been one of the dark places of the earth. The structure is scene-then-opening-remark, which is exactly what choice A names.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: scope shift — the passage does end with a remark from Marlow about the Thames, but the first half is not 'a sequence of events on the river' that has shaped his view, and the passage does not 'explain' how anything has shaped his view. The first half is a still scene at anchor; Marlow's remark is presented as his opening reflection, not as a conclusion drawn from prior events the narrator has reported.\n- Choice C: surface match — the passage does turn to the wider landscape of the Thames at dusk, which can make the second clause sound right; but the first clause is wrong. There is no quarrel aboard the yawl in the text. The men are silent, the wind is nearly calm, and Marlow himself says nothing at all for a long while. A choice that names a feature absent from the passage cannot describe its structure.\n- Choice D: inverse — this reverses the narrator's stance toward Marlow's remark. The narrator does not dismiss what Marlow says; the passage simply reports the remark as the first thing he has said after a long silence and gives it the weight of an opening observation. Treating the narrator as rejecting Marlow's words inverts the relationship the passage actually sets up between scene and speaker."
