          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Maryam Mirzakhani, a Stanford mathematician who died in 2017, is most often introduced as the first woman to receive the Fields Medal, the discipline's most distinguished award. That description, though accurate, captures a fact about her recognition rather than the substance of the work that earned it. Mirzakhani's central contribution, set out in a 2007 paper on simple geodesics and Weil-Petersson volumes, was a recursive procedure for computing the volumes of moduli spaces of Riemann surfaces with boundary. By counting simple closed geodesics on such surfaces and tracking how those counts behave as the boundary lengths vary, she derived a formula that expresses each volume in terms of volumes of lower-dimensional moduli spaces. The result reorganized a problem that had long resisted direct calculation and supplied a tool that subsequent work in dynamics and geometry has continued to draw on. Read in those terms, the significance of her career lies less in the historical milestone the medal marked than in the recursive bridge her theorem built between geodesics and the geometry of moduli space.",
