          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises. First, buried walls and foundations alter the soil and plants directly above them, producing faint contrasts in moisture and vegetation that multispectral imagery records in its narrow bands. Second, when looters dig pits into a buried tomb, that disturbance leaves a surface signature that appears in imagery taken after the digging but is absent from imagery of the same ground recorded before it. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that an analyst comparing multispectral images of the same site from different dates can use the appearance of a new surface signature in the later image to identify locations where buried remains have been newly disturbed by looting. Choice A states exactly that combined claim and stays within the scope the premises establish — same ground, two dates, a signature present in one image and absent from the other.\n\nThe inference combines both premises. Premise (1) alone tells us only that buried remains can be detected at all; it does not by itself license any claim about looting. Premise (2) alone, about looter pits leaving a surface signature, requires the comparison-across-dates structure of premise (1)'s imagery to support a conclusion about new disturbances. Only the joint claim — that comparing dated images reveals new looting at locations of buried remains — is licensed.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (Scope shift): Pushes a measured claim about comparing images from different dates into a much stronger claim about a single image taken on one date. Premise (2) specifies that the surface signature of looting appears in imagery taken after the digging but is absent from imagery of the same ground recorded before it; without an earlier image to set the baseline, a single later image cannot tell the analyst whether any given pit-shaped feature is new or old. The passage therefore does not license drawing a looting history from one image alone.\n- Choice C (Surface match): Sounds consistent with the passage's emphasis on what multispectral imagery can recover from buried remains, and dating buried structures is a familiar archaeological aim, which makes the claim feel plausible. But the premises do not license it. Premise (1) says only that buried walls and foundations produce faint surface contrasts that the narrow bands record, and premise (2) says that looter pits leave a fresh surface signature; neither tells us anything about how old the buried structures themselves are or that the imagery encodes that age. The choice is consistent-sounding but not derivable from the stated premises.\n- Choice D (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that looter pits leave a distinctive surface signature visible in imagery recorded after the digging; nothing in the text says that this signature is indistinguishable from that of natural erosion. Treating the narrow-band data as unable to separate the two processes inverts the asymmetry the second premise is built around."
