          "id": 239,
          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "When the astrophysicist Cecilia Payne-Gaposchkin defended her 1925 doctoral thesis at the Harvard College Observatory, she advanced two distinct claims drawn from the same spectral evidence. The first, which her senior colleagues largely accepted, held that observed differences among stellar spectra reflected differences in temperature and ionization rather than in chemical makeup. The second, which those colleagues urged her to soften, held that stars are composed overwhelmingly of hydrogen and helium — a finding that conflicted with the prevailing assumption that stars and Earth shared broadly similar compositions. The uneven reception of two conclusions resting on a single body of evidence suggests that, for the astronomers who pressed for that softening, ______",
          "question": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
