          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The passage sets up a controlled comparison. Payne-Gaposchkin's two claims rest on the same spectral evidence, yet her senior colleagues accepted the first (temperature and ionization explain spectral differences) while pressing her to soften the second (stars are overwhelmingly hydrogen and helium). The passage also identifies what distinguishes the second claim from the first: it conflicted with the prevailing assumption that stars and Earth shared broadly similar compositions. Because the evidence is held constant across the two conclusions, the differing reception cannot be explained by the evidence itself; it must be explained by something the astronomers brought to that evidence. The minimum inference the premises force is that, for those astronomers, how plausible a spectral inference appeared depended on more than the spectral data — it depended on whether the inference fit existing assumptions about stellar composition. Choice A states exactly this measured conclusion.\n\nAt this difficulty, the test is sufficient-vs-necessary: the premises license a claim about what shaped the astronomers' uneven reception, not a stronger claim about what the evidence could or could not establish on its own.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (Surface match): This is the load-bearing trap. The passage does say the senior astronomers urged Payne-Gaposchkin to soften her hydrogen-and-helium conclusion, and from that fact a reader might form the impression that the astronomers regarded the underlying evidence as inadequate. That impression is consistent with the passage but is not licensed by it. The premises explicitly note that the same astronomers accepted her first claim, which rests on the same spectral evidence; they cannot have judged that body of evidence insufficient to support either conclusion without contradicting that acceptance. A consistent-sounding hypothesis about the evidence is being passed off as a necessary inference.\n- Choice C (Scope shift): Strengthens a claim about how these astronomers received Payne-Gaposchkin's two conclusions into a universal procedural claim about what 'any inference' departing from the prevailing view would have required. The passage describes the reception of one specific case; it does not license a general rule about the kind of evidence needed to overturn the prevailing assumption.\n- Choice D (Inverse): Mischaracterizes the passage's logic. The temperature-and-ionization explanation is the claim the astronomers accepted, not a long-established view that the new spectral analysis could not unsettle. The passage frames both of Payne-Gaposchkin's conclusions as drawn from the same recent spectral analysis, so describing one of them as already so entrenched that no further evidence could disturb it runs against what the passage says about the source and timing of the two claims."
