          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises about what Manzanilla's isotopic evidence does and does not show. First, tooth enamel forms in childhood and locks in the chemical fingerprint of the local water and soil where a person grew up, so on Manzanilla's account the signatures function as a record of where a buried individual spent the first years of life rather than of how that person later came to live at Teotihuacan. Second, when signatures from co-residents of a single compound are compared with the regional geology of Mesoamerica, the chemistry repeatedly points to several different childhood regions. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that the residents of such a compound had grown up in distinct parts of Mesoamerica before sharing the compound, while the isotopic record alone does not specify the means by which they later came to live together. Choice A states exactly this hedged, two-part claim — the geographic difference of childhood origin from premise (2) and the explicit limit on what the isotopes can adjudicate from premise (1) — without overreaching the evidence.\n\nAt this difficulty, the test is sufficient-vs-necessary: the premises license a claim about distinct childhood origins (necessary inference), but they do not license a claim about the social mechanism that brought those residents together at Teotihuacan, however consistent such a mechanism may sound with a multiethnic reading of the city.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (Surface match): This is the load-bearing trap. A reader expecting the standard multiethnic conclusion about Teotihuacan may treat 'deliberately recruited... as part of a coordinated policy of urban resettlement' as the natural completion. But the inference is not licensed by the premises. Premise (1) explicitly limits what the isotopic signatures record — childhood geography, not the means by which a person later came to live at Teotihuacan — so a claim about deliberate recruitment by city authorities cannot be read off the chemistry alone, even though it is consistent with a multiethnic society. The choice asserts a sufficient mechanism (coordinated policy) where the premises establish only a necessary observation (different childhood regions).\n- Choice C (Scope shift): Pushes a modest claim about distinct childhood origins among compound co-residents into a universal claim about 'every major region of Mesoamerica' from which comparison chemistry was sampled. The premises support the inference that the chemistry pointed to several different childhood regions for residents of a single compound; they do not license the much stronger claim that any one compound represented every major sampled region.\n- Choice D (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that the chemistry repeatedly pointed to several different childhood regions for co-residents of a single compound, which is the opposite of having spent childhoods 'within a single shared region of central Mexico' whose chemistry the signatures 'uniformly matched.'"
