          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"gender; however,\". The blank sits at the boundary between two independent clauses, with the conjunctive adverb \"however\" signaling a contrast between them. The first clause — \"Earlier feminist accounts, she argues in *Black Feminist Thought* (1990), attended carefully to gender\" — has its own subject (\"accounts\") and verb (\"attended\") and can stand alone. The second clause — \"they tended to set aside the simultaneous workings of race and class, leaving the standpoint from which Black women theorize largely uncharted\" — has its own subject (\"they\") and verb (\"tended\") and can also stand alone. Standard English requires that two independent clauses joined by a conjunctive adverb (such as \"however,\" \"therefore,\" \"moreover,\" \"nevertheless\") take a semicolon before the conjunctive adverb and a comma after it: the semicolon marks the clause boundary that the comma alone cannot legally bridge, and the comma sets the conjunctive adverb off as a sentence-initial interrupter of the second clause. Choice B supplies that canonical \"; however,\" pattern.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"gender, however;\"): inverse — the two punctuation marks are placed in exactly the reversed positions. A reader who hears \"however\" as a parenthetical attached to the first clause might be tempted to set it off with a leading comma and then mark the clause boundary after \"however\" with a semicolon. But that pattern produces \"Earlier feminist accounts … attended carefully to gender, however; they tended to set aside…,\" which leaves \"however\" pointing backward inside the first clause and starts a new clause with no transition word at all. Standard English places the boundary mark before the conjunctive adverb that introduces the contrast, not after it.\n- Choice C (\"gender, however,\"): surface match — paired commas around \"however\" resemble the correct paired-comma offset that sets off short interrupters within a single clause. The pattern \"gender, however, …\" reads naturally if everything that follows is part of the same clause as \"gender\" — but it isn't. Because \"they tended to set aside…\" is a full independent clause, supplying a comma both before and after \"however\" creates a comma splice: two independent clauses joined by a comma, with \"however\" as a conjunctive adverb that cannot rescue the splice. (Conjunctive adverbs are not coordinating conjunctions; \"however\" is not in the FANBOYS set.) The mark before \"however\" must be promoted from a comma to a semicolon.\n- Choice D (\"gender however\"): scope shift — supplying no punctuation at all is a different SEC error. The two independent clauses run straight into each other and \"however\" is left without any boundary mark on either side, producing a fused (run-on) sentence in which the reader cannot tell whether \"however\" attaches to the end of the first clause or to the start of the second. Standard English requires both a clause-boundary mark before the conjunctive adverb and a comma after it.",
