          "explanation": "The blank is filled by a fronted modifier whose implicit subject must match the subject of the main clause that follows. The main-clause subject is the inanimate noun phrase 'Brooks's sonic archive.' Standard English requires that a fronted participial or infinitival modifier attach logically to that subject; otherwise, it dangles. Only choice A, the passive past-participle phrase 'Grounded in Spillers's account of dispossession,' attaches cleanly: an archive can be grounded in a prior account, so the modifier and the main-clause subject align. The result reads, 'Grounded in Spillers's account of dispossession, Brooks's sonic archive . . . recasts singing and staging . . . .'\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (B) 'Drawing on Spillers's account of dispossession,' is the surface-match distractor: a present-active participle is the form most students reach for to introduce a fronted modifier. But the implicit subject of 'drawing on' is whoever is doing the drawing — an animate agent — and the main-clause subject is 'Brooks's sonic archive,' an inanimate object that cannot draw on a prior account. The modifier dangles by attaching to the wrong subject.\n- (C) 'Having drawn on Spillers's account of dispossession,' is the scope-shift distractor: it commits the same dangling-modifier error as (B) but adds a second SEC concern by shifting aspect to the perfect participle. The implicit subject of 'having drawn on' is again an animate agent (the one who has drawn on the account); the inanimate subject 'Brooks's sonic archive' cannot perform that earlier action, so the modifier dangles in a new aspectual frame.\n- (D) 'To draw on Spillers's account of dispossession,' is the inverse distractor: it switches construction type entirely, from a participial modifier to an infinitive-of-purpose phrase. As a purpose phrase it requires an animate agent capable of acting in order to draw on the account, but the inanimate main-clause subject 'Brooks's sonic archive' cannot have purposes. The modifier dangles for the polar-opposite reason: not the wrong participial form, but the wrong construction type altogether.",
