          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "Sentence 1 reports what consumer ancestry-test companies advertise: that their DNA kits let customers confirm Native American heritage and support claims of tribal membership through saliva samples and probabilistic ancestry maps. Sentence 2 reports what Kim TallBear, an anthropologist at the University of Alberta, argues in Native American DNA: Tribal Belonging and the False Promise of Genetic Science (2013): tribal belonging is constituted by political and kinship relations rather than by genetic markers, so a consumer DNA result cannot establish citizenship in any tribal nation. The relation between the two sentences is direct opposition between two competing accounts of what fixes tribal membership — a marketing claim that genetic markers can confirm membership, and a scholarly argument that political and kinship relations alone constitute it. 'On the other hand,' is the CB-lexicon transition for setting one side of an opposed pair against the other, and is correct (B).\n\nThe cue that selects 'On the other hand,' is the explicit 'rather than by genetic markers' in sentence 2, which points back to and rejects the genetics-confirms-membership premise of sentence 1.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (A) 'Therefore,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) signals a causal consequence drawn from sentence 1. A reader might loosely hear 'because consumer DNA kits promise to confirm tribal membership, TallBear had to respond,' which gives 'Therefore,' a faint plausibility. But sentence 2 is not presented as a conclusion that follows from the marketing claim; it reports a scholarly argument that overturns it. The relation is opposition, not causal.\n- (C) 'Likewise,' (inverse — opposite logical move) signals that sentence 2 makes the same kind of point as sentence 1. That is exactly wrong here: TallBear is not echoing the ancestry-test companies' position that genetic markers can confirm tribal membership; she is rejecting it by arguing that political and kinship relations alone constitute tribal belonging. 'Likewise,' would point in the wrong direction.\n- (D) 'Moreover,' (scope shift — wrong relation) would frame TallBear's argument as an additional point reinforcing the marketing claim of sentence 1. But TallBear's position is not an additional confirmation that DNA testing can establish tribal membership; it is a competing account that displaces the genetic-markers premise. An addition transition cannot mark a sentence whose content opposes, rather than extends, the preceding claim.",
